How
===

High-level Overview
-------------------

The debugger uses tracepoint events for breakpoints. There's a single SESSION
object that keeps track of the whole debugging session. There's a session server
that has an event queue that processes events. When breakpoints are added (such
as calls to "debugger" or adding breakpoints interactively), The breakpoint objects
send events to the session server thread for processing. There, the session server
enters a session "subsession", stopping all threads and then defers to the UI for
interactivity with the client.

How to Make Ractor Friendly
---------------------------

The session object is difficult to work with because you can't share it across Ractors.
It would be nice if the session server were global still, but there was a ractor-friendly Queue (q_evt)
where we could send all the events so the session server could deal with them. Or, we could make this
session server its own ractor, and send events to it this way.

Edit: I tried this but it's very difficult to get this working. It's easier to have 1 session
object per Ractor.

What's Working
--------------
Using explicit "debugger" call:
c,n,p in main ractor
c,n,p in non-ractor
c,n,p from non-main ractor to main ractor

What's Not Working
------------------
* Stopping other ractors when in the debugger
  I wonder if there's a way to do this in pure Ruby. Probably not,
  but we could create a session automatically whenever a ractor begins, and
  then the line TracePoint would kick in and call `on_pause`. We would have to
  check if we're NOT in a subsession, and kick it off always if not.
  It might be easier to just do it via an ext or in Ruby itself, something like:
  Ractor.stop_all_others and Ractor.continue_all_others

What's Not Tested
-----------------
* All the other commands
* The server stuff
* Opening with VSCode, etc.



